IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`
IMPORT `sgia-interpretation-time`

-- Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024.
-- The source is a plain-text rendering (SCJA1969.txt), not the official text. Layer 1: the law, objectively; every name begins with the label of its provision.
-- scja2-part5-ca-civil.l4: Part 5 (Court of Appeal), Division 2, Civil jurisdiction: ss 53 to 60C.
-- JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (scja2)").
-- Twin: ss 35-45 and ss 53-60C say much the same about the two courts; each section is encoded on its own (ruling E1, option C) with the wording kept alike, and the differences are in the text, not in the encoding.
-- Dependency: sgia-interpretation-time.l4 (Interpretation Act 1965, s 50) is used for the 7 days of ss 41(10)(b) and 59(10)(b); it is copied unchanged and not edited.

§ `Part 5, Division 2 -- Civil jurisdiction`

§§ `s 53 -- Civil jurisdiction`

-- src:2980 | 53.—(1) This Division applies to the Court of Appeal in the
-- src:2981 | exercise of its civil jurisdiction.
@ref s 53(1) (src:2980-2981)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal is exercising its civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(1) this Division applies to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal is exercising its civil jurisdiction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "This Division applies to the Court of Appeal in the exercise of its civil jurisdiction"; outside that exercise the subsection does not say the Division is disapplied
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2990 | (a) any appeal against any decision made by the General
-- src:2991 | Division in any civil cause or matter in the exercise of its
-- src:2992 | original or appellate civil jurisdiction;
@ref s 53(2)(a) (src:2990-2992)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the matter is an appeal against that decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(a) the matter is an appeal against a decision of the General Division in a civil cause or matter` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): K1, whether a decision of the Singapore International Commercial Court is a decision "made by the General Division" is not settled by the text (s 18A calls it a division of the General Division; s 18J(4) and the Sixth Schedule para 1(f) treat it apart)
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the matter is an appeal against that decision`
           AND decision's `made by` EQUALS `General Division`
           AND decision's `nature of the proceeding` EQUALS `a civil proceeding`
           AND (decision's `in the exercise of` EQUALS `original jurisdiction`
                OR decision's `in the exercise of` EQUALS `appellate jurisdiction`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "consists of the following matters" closes the list, and limb (a) is an appeal against a decision made by the General Division in a civil cause or matter in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:2994 | (b) any appeal from the Appellate Division;
@ref s 53(2)(b) (src:2994)
GIVEN `the matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(b) the matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division` MEANS
    IF `the matter is an appeal from the Appellate Division`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "consists of the following matters" closes the list, and limb (b) is any appeal from the Appellate Division
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:2996 | (c) any appeal or other process that any written law provides is
-- src:2997 | to lie, or that is transferred in accordance with any written
-- src:2998 | law, to the Court of Appeal;
@ref s 53(2)(c) (src:2996-2998)
GIVEN `a written law provides that the appeal or process is to lie to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 29D(1) the appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(c) the matter is an appeal or other process that lies, or is transferred, to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    IF `a written law provides that the appeal or process is to lie to the Court of Appeal`
       OR `s 29D(1) the appeal was transferred to the Court of Appeal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "consists of the following matters" closes the list, and limb (c) is an appeal or other process that a written law provides is to lie, or that is transferred in accordance with a written law, to the Court of Appeal
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3004 | (i) a common question of law or fact arises in both the
-- src:3005 | application and a matter falling within the criminal
-- src:3006 | jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal;
@ref s 53(2)(d)(i) (src:3004-3006)
GIVEN `a common question of law or fact arises in both the application and a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(d)(i) the application raises a common question with a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` MEANS
    `a common question of law or fact arises in both the application and a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal`

-- src:3015 | (A) may affect any matter falling within the
-- src:3016 | criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal; or
@ref s 53(2)(d)(ii)(A) (src:3015-3016)
GIVEN `any relief claimed in the application may affect a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(d)(ii)(A) the relief claimed may affect a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` MEANS
    `any relief claimed in the application may affect a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal`

-- src:3018 | (B) may affect the outcome of any matter falling
-- src:3019 | within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of
-- src:3020 | Appeal.
@ref s 53(2)(d)(ii)(B) (src:3018-3020)
GIVEN `any relief claimed in the application may affect the outcome of a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(d)(ii)(B) the relief claimed may affect the outcome of a matter in the criminal jurisdiction` MEANS
    `any relief claimed in the application may affect the outcome of a matter falling within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal`

-- src:3000 | (d) any application (whether made to the General Division, the
-- src:3001 | Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal) to which either
-- src:3002 | or both of the following apply:
-- src:3004 | (i) a common question of law or fact arises in both the
-- src:3005 | application and a matter falling within the criminal
-- src:3006 | jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal;
-- src:3013 | (ii) any relief claimed in the application —
-- src:3015 | (A) may affect any matter falling within the
-- src:3016 | criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal; or
-- src:3018 | (B) may affect the outcome of any matter falling
-- src:3019 | within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of
-- src:3020 | Appeal.
@ref s 53(2)(d) (src:3000-3020)
GIVEN `the application is made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `limb (i)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `limb (ii)(A)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `limb (ii)(B)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(2)(d) the matter is an application to which either or both of the criminal-link limbs apply` MEANS
    IF `the application is made to the General Division, the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal` AND (`limb (i)` OR `limb (ii)(A)` OR `limb (ii)(B)`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "consists of the following matters" closes the list, and limb (d) is an application "to which either or both of the following apply"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:2985 | (2) The civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal consists of the
-- src:2986 | following matters, subject to the provisions of this Act or any other
-- src:2987 | written law regulating the terms and conditions upon which those
-- src:2988 | matters may be brought:
-- src:2990 | (a) any appeal against any decision made by the General
-- src:2991 | Division in any civil cause or matter in the exercise of its
-- src:2992 | original or appellate civil jurisdiction;
-- src:2994 | (b) any appeal from the Appellate Division;
-- src:2996 | (c) any appeal or other process that any written law provides is
-- src:2997 | to lie, or that is transferred in accordance with any written
-- src:2998 | law, to the Court of Appeal;
-- src:3000 | (d) any application (whether made to the General Division, the
-- src:3001 | Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal) to which either
-- src:3002 | or both of the following apply:
-- src:3004 | (i) a common question of law or fact arises in both the
-- src:3005 | application and a matter falling within the criminal
-- src:3006 | jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal;
-- src:3013 | (ii) any relief claimed in the application —
-- src:3015 | (A) may affect any matter falling within the
-- src:3016 | criminal jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal; or
-- src:3018 | (B) may affect the outcome of any matter falling
-- src:3019 | within the criminal jurisdiction of the Court of
-- src:3020 | Appeal.
@ref s 53(2) (src:2985-3020)
GIVEN `limb (a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (c)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (d)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53(2) the matter is one of the matters of the civil jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `limb (a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `limb (b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `limb (c)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `limb (d)` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "consists of the following matters" -- the list is closed and no limb holds
        IF `limb (a)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `limb (b)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `limb (c)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `limb (d)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): one limb is not settled by the text and no limb is met
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `s 54 -- Composition, further provisions`

-- src:3026 | 54.—(1) Despite section 50(1), a case before the Court of Appeal
-- src:3027 | may, if the Seventh Schedule so provides, be heard and decided by —
-- src:3029 | (a) either a single Judge or 2 Judges; or
-- src:3031 | (b) 2 Judges.
@ref s 54(1) (src:3026-3031)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who hear and decide the case` IS A NUMBER
      `sch 7 the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `sch 7 the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows` IS A MAYBE `Smaller panel`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54(1) the case may be heard and decided by that number of Judges` MEANS
    CONSIDER `sch 7 the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the Seventh Schedule so provides is answered by another part of this encoding (PLAN.md seam 14)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Seventh Schedule so provides" -- the Schedule does not so provide, so the "if" is not met (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (b))
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `sch 7 the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows`
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): what the Seventh Schedule provides is answered by another part of this encoding (PLAN.md seam 14)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST panel THEN
            CONSIDER panel
            WHEN `a single Judge or 2 Judges` THEN
                IF `the number of Judges who hear and decide the case` EQUALS 1 OR `the number of Judges who hear and decide the case` EQUALS 2
                THEN JUST TRUE
                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Seventh Schedule so provides" -- the Schedule provides for a single Judge or 2 Judges, not this number
                ELSE JUST FALSE
            WHEN `2 Judges` THEN
                IF `the number of Judges who hear and decide the case` EQUALS 2
                THEN JUST TRUE
                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Seventh Schedule so provides" -- the Schedule provides for 2 Judges, not this number
                ELSE JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): a single Judge alone is not one of the two limbs of this subsection (it is the Tenth Schedule's panel under s 60E(1))
            WHEN `a single Judge` THEN NOTHING

-- src:3035 | (2) A Judge must not sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide any
-- src:3036 | application to discharge or vary a direction or an order made by the
-- src:3037 | Judge under section 58(1).
@ref s 54(2) (src:3035-3037)
GIVEN `the application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order made by the Judge under section 58(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54(2) the Judge may sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide the application` MEANS
    IF `the application is to discharge or vary a direction or an order made by the Judge under section 58(1)`
    THEN
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A Judge must not sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide any application to discharge or vary a direction or an order made by the Judge under section 58(1)."
        JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition reaches only an application to discharge or vary the Judge's own direction or order under section 58(1)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The prohibition is derived from the answer above (ruling N2): the text names its bearer, "A Judge".
DECLARE `s 54(2) party` IS ONE OF `s 54(2) the Judge who made the direction or order`
DECLARE `s 54(2) act` IS ONE OF `s 54(2) sits in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide an application to discharge or vary the Judge's own direction or order under section 58(1)`

-- src:3035 | (2) A Judge must not sit in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide any
-- src:3036 | application to discharge or vary a direction or an order made by the
-- src:3037 | Judge under section 58(1).
@ref s 54(2) (src:3035-3037)
`s 54(2) the prohibition on the Judge` MEANS
    PARTY `s 54(2) the Judge who made the direction or order`
    SHANT `s 54(2) sits in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide an application to discharge or vary the Judge's own direction or order under section 58(1)`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `s 54(2) the Judge who made the direction or order` BECAUSE "a Judge sat in the Court of Appeal to hear an application to discharge or vary the Judge's own direction or order"

#TRACE `s 54(2) the prohibition on the Judge` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 54(2) the Judge who made the direction or order` DOES `s 54(2) sits in the Court of Appeal to hear or decide an application to discharge or vary the Judge's own direction or order under section 58(1)` AT 1

-- src:3041 | (3) Where the Court of Appeal is constituted by 3 or more Judges to
-- src:3042 | hear and decide a case and one or more of those Judges are unable for
-- src:3043 | any reason to continue exercising his or her functions as a Judge of
-- src:3044 | the Court of Appeal so constituted —
-- src:3046 | (a) the Court of Appeal must continue to hear and decide the
-- src:3047 | case if every party to the case consents and there are at least
-- src:3048 | 2 Judges remaining; and
-- src:3050 | (b) the case must be reheard if it cannot continue under
-- src:3051 | paragraph (a).
@ref s 54(3) (src:3041-3051)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue` IS A NUMBER
      `every party to the case consents` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54(3)(a) the Court of Appeal must continue to hear and decide the case` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the Court of Appeal is constituted by 3 or more Judges ... and one or more of those Judges are unable" -- the "where" is not met
        IF NOT (`the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` AT LEAST 3 AND `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue` AT LEAST 1) THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `every party to the case consents` AND (`the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` MINUS `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue`) AT LEAST 2 THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if every party to the case consents and there are at least 2 Judges remaining" -- the "if" is not met
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3050 | (b) the case must be reheard if it cannot continue under
-- src:3051 | paragraph (a).
@ref s 54(3)(b) (src:3050-3051)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue` IS A NUMBER
      `every party to the case consents` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54(3)(b) the case must be reheard` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the Court of Appeal is constituted by 3 or more Judges ... and one or more of those Judges are unable" -- the "where" is not met
        IF NOT (`the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` AT LEAST 3 AND `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue` AT LEAST 1) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if it cannot continue under paragraph (a)" -- the case can continue under paragraph (a)
        IF `every party to the case consents` AND (`the number of Judges who constituted the Court of Appeal` MINUS `the number of those Judges who are unable for any reason to continue`) AT LEAST 2 THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 55 -- Oral hearing not needed for appellate matters generally`

-- src:3057 | 55.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), the Court of Appeal may decide
-- src:3058 | any matter without hearing oral arguments, other than a matter
-- src:3059 | prescribed by the Rules of Court.
@ref s 55(1) (src:3057-3059)
GIVEN `the matter is one prescribed by the Rules of Court as needing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(1) the Court of Appeal may decide the matter without hearing oral arguments` MEANS
    IF `the matter is one prescribed by the Rules of Court as needing oral arguments`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "other than a matter prescribed by the Rules of Court" -- the Rules of Court are not among the sources, and the text does not say what happens to a prescribed matter
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:3066 | (2) Subsection (1) does not allow any part of a proceeding where
-- src:3067 | oral evidence is given to be conducted without an oral hearing, unless
-- src:3068 | all the parties consent.
@ref s 55(2) (src:3066-3068)
GIVEN `oral evidence is given in that part of the proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(2) the part of the proceeding where oral evidence is given may be conducted without an oral hearing` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "any part of a proceeding where oral evidence is given" -- the "where" is not met (no oral evidence is given in this part), so the prohibition is not triggered and the text is silent on whether the part may be conducted without an oral hearing (that is for subsection (1)); the bar-phrased function below asks the prohibition's own question and answers JUST FALSE here (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (b))
        IF NOT `oral evidence is given in that part of the proceeding` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless all the parties consent" -- the unless is met, which lifts the bar without itself granting the allowance (subsection (1) does that)
        IF `all the parties consent` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (1) does not allow any part of a proceeding where oral evidence is given to be conducted without an oral hearing, unless all the parties consent."
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3066 | (2) Subsection (1) does not allow any part of a proceeding where
-- src:3067 | oral evidence is given to be conducted without an oral hearing, unless
-- src:3068 | all the parties consent.
@ref s 55(2) (src:3066-3068)
GIVEN `oral evidence is given in that part of the proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(2) the bar on conducting the part of the proceeding without an oral hearing applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any part of a proceeding where oral evidence is given" -- the "where" is not met, so the bar does not apply
        IF NOT `oral evidence is given in that part of the proceeding` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless all the parties consent" -- the unless is met, so the bar is lifted
        IF `all the parties consent` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:3070 | (3) Subject to subsection (4), the Court of Appeal may, in any
-- src:3071 | matter that the court may decide without hearing oral arguments,
-- src:3072 | direct that the matter be heard in an asynchronous manner by
-- src:3073 | exchange of written correspondence with the party or parties using
-- src:3074 | such means of communication as directed by the court.
@ref s 55(3) (src:3070-3074)
GIVEN `the matter is one that the Court of Appeal may decide without hearing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(3) the Court of Appeal may direct that the matter be heard in an asynchronous manner by exchange of written correspondence` MEANS
    IF `the matter is one that the Court of Appeal may decide without hearing oral arguments`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is "in any matter that the court may decide without hearing oral arguments", and is "subject to subsection (4)"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3076 | (4) The Court of Appeal must not hear a matter in an asynchronous
-- src:3077 | manner if to do so would be inconsistent with the court’s duty to
-- src:3078 | ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties.
@ref s 55(4) (src:3076-3078)
GIVEN `to hear it in that manner would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(4) the bar on hearing the matter in an asynchronous manner applies to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    IF `to hear it in that manner would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if to do so would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties" -- the "if" is not met, so the bar does not apply (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (b))
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- The prohibition is derived from the answer above (ruling N2): the text names its bearer, "The Court of Appeal".
DECLARE `s 55(4) party` IS ONE OF `s 55(4) the Court of Appeal`
DECLARE `s 55(4) act` IS ONE OF `s 55(4) hears the matter in an asynchronous manner where that would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties`

-- src:3076 | (4) The Court of Appeal must not hear a matter in an asynchronous
-- src:3077 | manner if to do so would be inconsistent with the court’s duty to
-- src:3078 | ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties.
@ref s 55(4) (src:3076-3078)
`s 55(4) the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    PARTY `s 55(4) the Court of Appeal`
    SHANT `s 55(4) hears the matter in an asynchronous manner where that would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `s 55(4) the Court of Appeal` BECAUSE "the Court of Appeal heard a matter in an asynchronous manner although that was inconsistent with its duty to ensure fairness"

#TRACE `s 55(4) the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 55(4) the Court of Appeal` DOES `s 55(4) hears the matter in an asynchronous manner where that would be inconsistent with the court's duty to ensure that the proceedings are conducted fairly to all parties` AT 1

-- src:3080 | (5) To avoid doubt, this section does not affect the power of the
-- src:3081 | Court of Appeal to hear oral arguments before deciding any matter
-- src:3082 | that may be decided without hearing oral arguments.
@ref s 55(5) (src:3080-3082)
GIVEN `the matter is one that may be decided without hearing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 55(5) the Court of Appeal may hear oral arguments before deciding the matter` MEANS
    IF `the matter is one that may be decided without hearing oral arguments`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "To avoid doubt" the section does not affect the power to hear oral arguments before deciding "any matter that may be decided without hearing oral arguments"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 56 -- Summary dismissal of certain matters`

-- src:3088 | 56.—(1) The Court of Appeal may summarily dismiss any appeal
-- src:3089 | or application on its own motion if the Court of Appeal is satisfied of
-- src:3090 | any of the following:
-- src:3092 | (a) the Court of Appeal does not have the jurisdiction to hear
-- src:3093 | and determine the appeal or application;
-- src:3095 | (b) the Court of Appeal has already decided every issue in the
-- src:3096 | appeal or application in an earlier matter in which the
-- src:3097 | appellant or applicant was involved, and the appeal or
-- src:3098 | application therefore has no merit;
-- src:3100 | (c) such conditions as may be prescribed by the Rules of Court
-- src:3101 | are met.
@ref s 56(1) (src:3088-3101)
GIVEN `it acts on its own motion` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is satisfied that it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine the appeal or application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 56(1)(b) every issue was already decided in an earlier matter involving the appellant or applicant, and the appeal or application therefore has no merit` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 56(1) the Court of Appeal may summarily dismiss the appeal or application on its own motion` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is given "on its own motion"
        IF NOT `it acts on its own motion` THEN NOTHING
        -- limb (a): no jurisdiction; limb (b): the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal has already decided every issue and the appeal has no merit (the assessment is taken as a fact: PLAN.md difficulty 7); limb (c): the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court
        IF `it is satisfied that it does not have the jurisdiction to hear and determine the appeal or application`
           OR `s 56(1)(b) every issue was already decided in an earlier matter involving the appellant or applicant, and the appeal or application therefore has no merit`
           OR `it is satisfied that the conditions prescribed by the Rules of Court are met`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Court of Appeal is satisfied of any of the following" -- it is satisfied of none of them
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3105 | (2) Before summarily dismissing any appeal or application under
-- src:3106 | subsection (1), the Court of Appeal must —
-- src:3108 | (a) give the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to
-- src:3109 | show cause why the appeal or application should not be
-- src:3110 | summarily dismissed; and
-- src:3117 | (b) consider any representations made by the appellant or
-- src:3118 | applicant.
@ref s 56(2) (src:3105-3118)
GIVEN `it gave the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause why the appeal or application should not be summarily dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it considered any representations made by the appellant or applicant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 56(2) the Court of Appeal has met the requirements before summarily dismissing, as the section requires` MEANS
    IF `it gave the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause why the appeal or application should not be summarily dismissed` AND `it considered any representations made by the appellant or applicant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "Before summarily dismissing any appeal or application under subsection (1), the Court of Appeal must -- (a) give the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause ...; and (b) consider any representations" -- one of the two is not done -- a bare "must" not done: the text states the duty and does not say what follows
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `s 56(2) party` IS ONE OF `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal`
DECLARE `s 56(2) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 56(2)(a) gives the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause`
    `s 56(2)(b) considers the representations made`

-- src:3105 | (2) Before summarily dismissing any appeal or application under
-- src:3106 | subsection (1), the Court of Appeal must —
-- src:3108 | (a) give the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to
-- src:3109 | show cause why the appeal or application should not be
-- src:3110 | summarily dismissed; and
-- src:3117 | (b) consider any representations made by the appellant or
-- src:3118 | applicant.
@ref s 56(2) (src:3105-3118)
`s 56(2) the duties before summary dismissal` MEANS
    PARTY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal`
    MUST `s 56(2)(a) gives the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause`
    HENCE (
        PARTY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal`
        MUST `s 56(2)(b) considers the representations made`
        LEST BREACH BY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal` BECAUSE "the representations were not considered before summary dismissal"
    )
    LEST BREACH BY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal` BECAUSE "no reasonable opportunity to show cause was given before summary dismissal"

#TRACE `s 56(2) the duties before summary dismissal` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal` DOES `s 56(2)(a) gives the appellant or applicant a reasonable opportunity to show cause` AT 1
    PARTY `s 56(2) the Court of Appeal` DOES `s 56(2)(b) considers the representations made` AT 2

-- src:3122 | (3) The Court of Appeal may exercise its powers under this section
-- src:3123 | without hearing oral arguments.
@ref s 56(3) (src:3122-3123)
GIVEN `it is exercising its powers under this section` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 56(3) the Court of Appeal may exercise its powers under this section without hearing oral arguments` MEANS
    IF `it is exercising its powers under this section`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the grant is of "its powers under this section"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3127 | (4) In this section, “appeal” includes part of an appeal and
-- src:3128 | “application” includes part of an application.
@ref s 56(4) (src:3127-3128)
GIVEN `it is an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is part of an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is part of an application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 56(4) the thing is an appeal or an application for the purposes of this section` MEANS
    IF `it is an appeal` OR `it is part of an appeal` OR `it is an application` OR `it is part of an application`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "appeal" includes part of an appeal and "application" includes part of an application; the text does not say that nothing else counts
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 57 -- Applications`

-- src:3134 | 57. Where an application may be made either to the Court of
-- src:3135 | Appeal or to another court, it must first be made to the other court.
@ref s 57 (src:3134-3135)
GIVEN `the application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 57 the application must first be made to the other court` MEANS
    IF `the application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court" -- the "where" is not met, so this section does not require the application to be made first to another court
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3134 | 57. Where an application may be made either to the Court of
-- src:3135 | Appeal or to another court, it must first be made to the other court.
@ref s 57 (src:3134-3135)
GIVEN `the application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the other court` IS A Court
GIVETH A MAYBE Court
`s 57 the court to which the application must first be made` MEANS
    IF `the application may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court`
    THEN JUST `the other court`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the section speaks only of an application that may be made either to the Court of Appeal or to another court
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 58 -- Incidental directions and interim orders`

DECLARE `s 58(1) kind of direction or order` IS ONE OF
    `s 58(1)(a) a direction or order incidental to the pending matter not involving the decision of the pending matter`
    `s 58(1)(b) an interim order to prevent prejudice to the claims of the parties pending the determination of the pending matter`
    `s 58(1)(c) an order for security for costs, or for the dismissal of the pending matter for default in furnishing security so ordered`
    `s 58(1) another direction or order`

-- src:3141 | 58.—(1) The Court of Appeal may make one or more of the
-- src:3142 | following directions and orders in any appeal or application pending
-- src:3143 | before it (called in this section the pending matter):
-- src:3145 | (a) any direction or order incidental to the pending matter not
-- src:3146 | involving the decision of the pending matter;
-- src:3148 | (b) any interim order to prevent prejudice to the claims of the
-- src:3149 | parties pending the determination of the pending matter;
-- src:3151 | (c) any order for security for costs, and for the dismissal of the
-- src:3152 | pending matter for default in furnishing security so
-- src:3153 | ordered.
@ref s 58(1) (src:3141-3153)
GIVEN `the appeal or application is pending before the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      kind IS A `s 58(1) kind of direction or order`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(1) the Court of Appeal may make the direction or order in the pending matter` MEANS
    IF NOT `the appeal or application is pending before the Court of Appeal`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is "in any appeal or application pending before it"
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER kind
        WHEN `s 58(1)(a) a direction or order incidental to the pending matter not involving the decision of the pending matter` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `s 58(1)(b) an interim order to prevent prejudice to the claims of the parties pending the determination of the pending matter` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `s 58(1)(c) an order for security for costs, or for the dismissal of the pending matter for default in furnishing security so ordered` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): the subsection lists the directions and orders it gives "the following" and does not close the list with "only"
        WHEN `s 58(1) another direction or order` THEN NOTHING

-- src:3157 | (2) A direction or an order under subsection (1) may be made by the
-- src:3158 | Court of Appeal on its own motion or on the application of a party.
@ref s 58(2) (src:3157-3158)
GIVEN `it acts on its own motion` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a party applies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(2) the Court of Appeal may make the direction or order on its own motion or on the application of a party` MEANS
    IF `it acts on its own motion` OR `a party applies`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection names two ways a direction or order may come about and says nothing of any other
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3162 | (3) A direction or an order under subsection (1) may be made by
-- src:3163 | 2 Judges.
@ref s 58(3) (src:3162-3163)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who make the direction or order` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(3) the direction or order may be made by that number of Judges` MEANS
    IF `the number of Judges who make the direction or order` EQUALS 2
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection says "2 Judges"; subsection (4) deals with a single Judge
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3167 | (4) A direction or an order under subsection (1) may also be made
-- src:3168 | by a single Judge, in which case the following provisions apply:
-- src:3175 | (a) the direction or order may be varied or discharged by
-- src:3176 | 2 other Judges; but
-- src:3178 | (b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order
-- src:3179 | may only be made with the permission of the single Judge
-- src:3180 | or any other Judge, and a decision by any Judge to give or
-- src:3181 | refuse permission is final.
@ref s 58(4) (src:3167-3181)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who make the direction or order` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(4) the direction or order may be made by a single Judge` MEANS
    IF `the number of Judges who make the direction or order` EQUALS 1
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection says "a single Judge"; subsection (3) deals with 2 Judges
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3175 | (a) the direction or order may be varied or discharged by
-- src:3176 | 2 other Judges; but
@ref s 58(4)(a) (src:3175-3176)
GIVEN `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the number of other Judges who vary or discharge it` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(4)(a) the direction or order may be varied or discharged by those Judges` MEANS
    IF `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` AND `the number of other Judges who vary or discharge it` EQUALS 2
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph says the direction or order made by a single Judge "may be varied or discharged by 2 other Judges"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3178 | (b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order
-- src:3179 | may only be made with the permission of the single Judge
-- src:3180 | or any other Judge, and a decision by any Judge to give or
-- src:3181 | refuse permission is final.
@ref s 58(4)(b) (src:3178-3181)
GIVEN `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the single Judge or another Judge has given permission to apply to vary or discharge it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(4)(b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order may be made` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph applies to a direction or order made by a single Judge
        IF NOT `the direction or order was made by a single Judge` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the single Judge or another Judge has given permission to apply to vary or discharge it` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may only be made with the permission of the single Judge or any other Judge"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3178 | (b) an application to vary or discharge the direction or order
-- src:3179 | may only be made with the permission of the single Judge
-- src:3180 | or any other Judge, and a decision by any Judge to give or
-- src:3181 | refuse permission is final.
@ref s 58(4)(b) (src:3178-3181)
GIVEN `the decision is a decision by a Judge to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(4)(b) the decision to give or refuse permission is final` MEANS
    IF `the decision is a decision by a Judge to give or refuse permission to apply to vary or discharge the direction or order`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision by any Judge to give or refuse permission is final" -- the text says nothing of a decision of another kind
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3187 | (5) Subsections (3) and (4) apply despite section 50(1).
@ref s 58(5) (src:3187)
GIVEN `the direction or order is made under subsection (3) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(5) section 50(1) does not stand in the way of the direction or order` MEANS
    IF `the direction or order is made under subsection (3) or (4)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Subsections (3) and (4) apply despite section 50(1)"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 59 -- Hearing of appeals`

-- src:3193 | 59.—(1) Appeals to the Court of Appeal are to be by way of
-- src:3194 | rehearing.
@ref s 59(1) (src:3193-3194)
GIVEN `it is an appeal to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(1) the appeal is by way of rehearing` MEANS
    IF `it is an appeal to the Court of Appeal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Appeals to the Court of Appeal are to be by way of rehearing"; the subsection says nothing of any other proceeding
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3198 | (2) In hearing and deciding an appeal, the Court of Appeal has all
-- src:3199 | the powers and duties, as to amendment or otherwise, of the court or
-- src:3200 | tribunal from which the appeal was brought.
@ref s 59(2) (src:3198-3200)
GIVEN `it is hearing and deciding an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the power or duty is one of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought, as to amendment or otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(2) the Court of Appeal has the power or duty of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought` MEANS
    IF `it is hearing and deciding an appeal` AND `the power or duty is one of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought, as to amendment or otherwise`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In hearing and deciding an appeal" and "of the court or tribunal from which the appeal was brought"
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `s 59(3) manner of receiving further evidence` IS ONE OF
    `s 59(3)(a) by oral examination in court`
    `s 59(3)(b) by affidavit`
    `s 59(3)(c) by deposition taken before an examiner or a commissioner`
    `s 59(3)(d) in any other manner that the Court of Appeal allows`
    `s 59(3) in a manner that the Court of Appeal does not allow`

-- src:3204 | (3) The Court of Appeal may receive further evidence —
-- src:3206 | (a) by oral examination in court;
-- src:3208 | (b) by affidavit;
-- src:3210 | (c) by deposition taken before an examiner or a commissioner;
-- src:3211 | or
-- src:3213 | (d) in any other manner that the Court of Appeal allows.
@ref s 59(3) (src:3204-3213)
GIVEN manner IS A `s 59(3) manner of receiving further evidence`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) the Court of Appeal may receive further evidence in that manner` MEANS
    CONSIDER manner
    WHEN `s 59(3)(a) by oral examination in court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 59(3)(b) by affidavit` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 59(3)(c) by deposition taken before an examiner or a commissioner` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 59(3)(d) in any other manner that the Court of Appeal allows` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (d) reaches "any other manner that the Court of Appeal allows"; the text does not say what follows where the court does not allow the manner
    WHEN `s 59(3) in a manner that the Court of Appeal does not allow` THEN NOTHING

-- src:3217 | (4) Except as provided in subsection (5), such further evidence may
-- src:3218 | be given to the Court of Appeal only with the permission of the Court
-- src:3219 | of Appeal and on special grounds.
@ref s 59(4) (src:3217-3219)
GIVEN `subsection (5) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court of Appeal has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there are special grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(4) the further evidence may be given to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Except as provided in subsection (5)" -- where subsection (5) applies this subsection does not govern
        IF `subsection (5) applies` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the Court of Appeal has given permission` AND `there are special grounds` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may be given to the Court of Appeal only with the permission of the Court of Appeal and on special grounds"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3217 | (4) Except as provided in subsection (5), such further evidence may
-- src:3218 | be given to the Court of Appeal only with the permission of the Court
-- src:3219 | of Appeal and on special grounds.
@ref s 59(4) (src:3217-3219)
GIVEN `subsection (5) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court of Appeal has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there are special grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(4) the bar on giving the further evidence to the Court of Appeal applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Except as provided in subsection (5)" -- subsection (5) applies, so the bar does not
        IF `subsection (5) applies` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "only with the permission of the Court of Appeal and on special grounds" -- both are present, so the bar is lifted
        IF `the Court of Appeal has given permission` AND `there are special grounds` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:3225 | (5) Such further evidence may be given to the Court of Appeal
-- src:3226 | without permission if the evidence relates to matters occurring after
-- src:3227 | the date of the decision appealed against.
@ref s 59(5) (src:3225-3227)
GIVEN `the evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(5) the further evidence may be given to the Court of Appeal without permission` MEANS
    IF `the evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the evidence relates to matters occurring after the date of the decision appealed against" -- the "if" is not met, so this subsection does not allow the evidence without permission
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3238 | (6) The Court of Appeal may draw any inference of fact, give any
-- src:3239 | judgment and make any order.
@ref s 59(6) (src:3238-3239)
GIVEN `it is hearing and deciding an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(6) the Court of Appeal may draw any inference of fact, give any judgment and make any order` MEANS
    IF `it is hearing and deciding an appeal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is one of the powers "in this section" about the hearing of appeals
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3245 | (a) may be exercised in relation to any part of the decision
-- src:3246 | appealed against, including any part of the decision
-- src:3247 | appealed against to which the appeal does not relate; and
@ref s 59(7)(a) (src:3245-3247)
GIVEN `the power is one of the powers in this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the exercise is in relation to a part of the decision appealed against, including a part to which the appeal does not relate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(7)(a) the power may be exercised in relation to that part of the decision appealed against` MEANS
    IF `the power is one of the powers in this section` AND `the exercise is in relation to a part of the decision appealed against, including a part to which the appeal does not relate`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The powers in this section" and "any part of the decision appealed against"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3249 | (b) may be exercised in favour of any party to the decision
-- src:3250 | appealed against, including any party to the decision
-- src:3251 | appealed against who has not appealed against the
-- src:3252 | decision.
@ref s 59(7)(b) (src:3249-3252)
GIVEN `the power is one of the powers in this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the exercise is in favour of a party to the decision appealed against, including a party who has not appealed against it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(7)(b) the power may be exercised in favour of that party` MEANS
    IF `the power is one of the powers in this section` AND `the exercise is in favour of a party to the decision appealed against, including a party who has not appealed against it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The powers in this section" and "any party to the decision appealed against"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3256 | (8) Where an application for permission to adduce further evidence
-- src:3257 | in an appeal before the Court of Appeal is heard and decided by a
-- src:3258 | single Judge or 2 Judges, any party may request the full panel of the
-- src:3259 | Court of Appeal hearing the appeal to rehear arguments in respect of
-- src:3260 | the application for permission to adduce further evidence.
@ref s 59(8) (src:3256-3260)
GIVEN `the application for permission to adduce further evidence was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(8) a party may request the full panel to rehear arguments on the application for permission to adduce further evidence` MEANS
    IF `the application for permission to adduce further evidence was heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an application for permission to adduce further evidence in an appeal before the Court of Appeal is heard and decided by a single Judge or 2 Judges" -- the "where" is not met
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3264 | (9) No request may be made under subsection (8) in respect of an
-- src:3265 | application for permission to adduce further evidence in an appeal
-- src:3266 | where the Court of Appeal that first decided the application is
-- src:3267 | constituted —
-- src:3269 | (a) by 2 Judges, where the Court of Appeal hearing the appeal
-- src:3270 | is constituted by 2 Judges; or
-- src:3272 | (b) by 3 or more Judges.
@ref s 59(9) (src:3264-3272)
GIVEN `the number of Judges who first decided the application` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of Judges hearing the appeal` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(9) the bar on a request under subsection (8) applies` MEANS
    IF (`the number of Judges who first decided the application` EQUALS 2 AND `the number of Judges hearing the appeal` EQUALS 2) OR `the number of Judges who first decided the application` AT LEAST 3
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the Court of Appeal that first decided the application is constituted -- (a) by 2 Judges, where the Court of Appeal hearing the appeal is constituted by 2 Judges; or (b) by 3 or more Judges" -- the "where" is not met, so the bar does not apply (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (b))
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3279 | (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the
-- src:3280 | decision on the application for permission to adduce
-- src:3281 | further evidence is extracted;
@ref s 59(10)(a) (src:3279-3281)
GIVEN `the judgment or order had already been extracted when the request was made` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(10)(a) the request is made before the time at which the judgment or order is extracted` MEANS
    IF `the judgment or order had already been extracted when the request was made`
    THEN
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "must be made before the earlier of the following: (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the decision ... is extracted" -- the time has passed
        JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:3283 | (b) the expiration of 7 days after the date on which the decision
-- src:3284 | on the application for permission to adduce further
-- src:3285 | evidence is made.
@ref s 59(10)(b) (src:3283-3285)
GIVEN `the date on which the decision on the application for permission to adduce further evidence is made` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 59(10)(b) the last day of the 7 days after the date on which the decision is made` MEANS
    -- Fork (NOTES.md): the 7 days "after" the date are counted from the day after the decision (s 50(a) of the Interpretation Act excludes the day of the event), and s 50(b) is applied to the last day.
    -- The staged module's `the last day of a period` answers NOTHING for the counting word "after"; this rule uses its s 50(b) function on the day that is 7 days after the date, as the module's own comment prescribes.
    `the last day of the period once section 50(b) is applied`
        (`the date on which the decision on the application for permission to adduce further evidence is made` PLUS `the Act's number of days for ss 41(10)(b) and 59(10)(b)`)
        Days `the public holidays` FALSE

-- src:3276 | (10) A request under subsection (8) must be made before the earlier
-- src:3277 | of the following:
-- src:3279 | (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the
-- src:3280 | decision on the application for permission to adduce
-- src:3281 | further evidence is extracted;
-- src:3283 | (b) the expiration of 7 days after the date on which the decision
-- src:3284 | on the application for permission to adduce further
-- src:3285 | evidence is made.
@ref s 59(10) (src:3276-3285)
GIVEN `the date on which the request is made` IS A DATE
      `the judgment or order had already been extracted when the request was made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date on which the decision on the application for permission to adduce further evidence is made` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(10) the request under subsection (8) is made in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 59(10)(b) the last day of the 7 days after the date on which the decision is made` `the date on which the decision on the application for permission to adduce further evidence is made` `the public holidays`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Interpretation Act module gives no last day
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST lastDay THEN
        BRANCH
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "must be made before the earlier of the following" -- the judgment or order had already been extracted, or the 7 days had expired
            IF `the judgment or order had already been extracted when the request was made` THEN JUST FALSE
            IF `the date on which the request is made` GREATER THAN lastDay THEN JUST FALSE
            OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `s 59(8) party` IS ONE OF `s 59(8) a party to the appeal`
DECLARE `s 59(8) act` IS ONE OF `s 59(8) requests the full panel to rehear arguments`

-- The clock of this rule counts days from the day the decision on the application for permission is made (day 0); the other limb, extraction of the judgment or order, is a fact and is in the decision function above.
-- src:3276 | (10) A request under subsection (8) must be made before the earlier
-- src:3277 | of the following:
-- src:3279 | (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the
-- src:3280 | decision on the application for permission to adduce
-- src:3281 | further evidence is extracted;
-- src:3283 | (b) the expiration of 7 days after the date on which the decision
-- src:3284 | on the application for permission to adduce further
-- src:3285 | evidence is made.
@ref s 59(10) (src:3276-3285)
`s 59(8) and (10) the request` MEANS
    PARTY `s 59(8) a party to the appeal`
    MAY `s 59(8) requests the full panel to rehear arguments`
    WITHIN 7
    HENCE FULFILLED

#TRACE `s 59(8) and (10) the request` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 59(8) a party to the appeal` DOES `s 59(8) requests the full panel to rehear arguments` AT 5

-- src:3294 | (11) After rehearing arguments on a request mentioned in
-- src:3295 | subsection (8), the Court of Appeal may affirm, vary or set aside
-- src:3296 | the decision on the application for permission to adduce further
-- src:3297 | evidence.
@ref s 59(11) (src:3294-3297)
GIVEN `it has reheard arguments on a request mentioned in subsection (8)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(11) the Court of Appeal may affirm, vary or set aside the decision on the application for permission to adduce further evidence` MEANS
    IF `it has reheard arguments on a request mentioned in subsection (8)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power arises "After rehearing arguments on a request mentioned in subsection (8)"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 60 -- Costs of appeal`

-- src:3303 | 60. The Court of Appeal may make any order as to the costs of an
-- src:3304 | appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against.
@ref s 60 (src:3303-3304)
GIVEN `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60 the Court of Appeal may make the order as to costs` MEANS
    IF `the order is as to the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is over "the costs of an appeal or of the proceedings relating to the decision appealed against"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 60A -- New trial`

-- src:3313 | (a) any matter that has been tried by the General Division in
-- src:3314 | the exercise of the original or appellate civil jurisdiction of
-- src:3315 | the General Division; or
@ref s 60A(1)(a) (src:3313-3315)
GIVEN `the court that tried the matter` IS A Court
      `the jurisdiction it exercised` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
      `the matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(1)(a) the Court of Appeal may order a new trial of a matter tried by the General Division` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): K1, whether the Singapore International Commercial Court is "the General Division" is not settled by the text (s 18A, s 18J(4))
        IF `the court that tried the matter` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is over a matter tried "in the exercise of the original or appellate civil jurisdiction" of the General Division; a matter tried in the exercise of criminal jurisdiction is outside the scope, and the text has no "only" (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (c))
        IF NOT `the matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the court that tried the matter` EQUALS `General Division`
           AND (`the jurisdiction it exercised` EQUALS `original jurisdiction` OR `the jurisdiction it exercised` EQUALS `appellate jurisdiction`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is over "any matter that has been tried by the General Division in the exercise of the original or appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:3317 | (b) any matter that has been tried by the Appellate Division in
-- src:3318 | the exercise of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate
-- src:3319 | Division.
@ref s 60A(1)(b) (src:3317-3319)
GIVEN `the court that tried the matter` IS A Court
      `the matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(1)(b) the Court of Appeal may order a new trial of a matter tried by the Appellate Division` MEANS
    IF `the court that tried the matter` EQUALS `Appellate Division` AND `the matter was tried in the exercise of civil jurisdiction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is over "any matter that has been tried by the Appellate Division in the exercise of the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3323 | (2) A new trial must not be ordered on the ground of an improper
-- src:3324 | admission or rejection of evidence, unless the improper admission or
-- src:3325 | rejection has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice.
@ref s 60A(2) (src:3323-3325)
GIVEN `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(2) a new trial may be ordered on that ground` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition is on a new trial "on the ground of an improper admission or rejection of evidence"
        IF NOT `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless the improper admission or rejection has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice" -- the unless is met, which lifts the bar without itself granting a new trial (subsection (1) does that)
        IF `it has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A new trial must not be ordered on the ground of an improper admission or rejection of evidence, unless the improper admission or rejection has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice."
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3323 | (2) A new trial must not be ordered on the ground of an improper
-- src:3324 | admission or rejection of evidence, unless the improper admission or
-- src:3325 | rejection has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice.
@ref s 60A(2) (src:3323-3325)
GIVEN `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(2) the bar on a new trial on that ground applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition is on a new trial "on the ground of an improper admission or rejection of evidence"
        IF NOT `the ground is an improper admission or rejection of evidence` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the improper admission or rejection has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice" -- the unless is met, so the bar is lifted
        IF `it has caused a substantial wrong or a miscarriage of justice` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:3333 | (a) give final judgment in respect of the unaffected part of the
-- src:3334 | case or the unaffected parties to the case; and
@ref s 60A(3)(a) (src:3333-3334)
GIVEN `the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affects only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(3)(a) the Court of Appeal may give final judgment in respect of the unaffected part of the case or the unaffected parties` MEANS
    IF `the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affects only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice mentioned in subsection (2) affects only part of a case, or only one or some of the parties to the case" -- the "if" is not met
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3336 | (b) direct a new trial in respect of the affected part of the case
-- src:3337 | or the affected parties to the case.
@ref s 60A(3)(b) (src:3336-3337)
GIVEN `the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affects only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(3)(b) the Court of Appeal may direct a new trial in respect of the affected part of the case or the affected parties` MEANS
    IF `the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice affects only part of the case, or only one or some of the parties`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If the substantial wrong or miscarriage of justice mentioned in subsection (2) affects only part of a case, or only one or some of the parties to the case" -- the "if" is not met
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3341 | (4) A new trial may be ordered on any question without interfering
-- src:3342 | with the finding or decision of the General Division or the Appellate
-- src:3343 | Division (as the case may be) on any other question.
@ref s 60A(4) (src:3341-3343)
GIVEN `the new trial is ordered on a question` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60A(4) the new trial may be ordered on that question without interfering with the finding or decision on any other question` MEANS
    IF `the new trial is ordered on a question`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks of a new trial "on any question"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 60B -- Immaterial errors`

-- src:3354 | 60B. The Court of Appeal may not reverse or substantially vary a
-- src:3355 | decision appealed against, or order a new trial, on account of any
-- src:3356 | error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits of the
-- src:3357 | decision appealed against or the jurisdiction of the court whose
-- src:3358 | decision is appealed against.
@ref s 60B (src:3354-3358)
GIVEN `it is asked to reverse or substantially vary the decision, or to order a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the error, defect or irregularity does not affect the merits of the decision appealed against or the jurisdiction of the court whose decision is appealed against` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60B the Court of Appeal may reverse or substantially vary the decision, or order a new trial, on account of the error` MEANS
    IF `it is asked to reverse or substantially vary the decision, or to order a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity`
       AND `the error, defect or irregularity does not affect the merits of the decision appealed against or the jurisdiction of the court whose decision is appealed against`
    THEN
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Court of Appeal may not reverse or substantially vary a decision ... or order a new trial, on account of any error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits ... or the jurisdiction"
        JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is on an error that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The prohibition is derived from the answer above (ruling N2): the text names its bearer, "The Court of Appeal".
DECLARE `s 60B party` IS ONE OF `s 60B the Court of Appeal`
DECLARE `s 60B act` IS ONE OF `s 60B reverses or substantially varies the decision, or orders a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction`

-- src:3354 | 60B. The Court of Appeal may not reverse or substantially vary a
-- src:3355 | decision appealed against, or order a new trial, on account of any
-- src:3356 | error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits of the
-- src:3357 | decision appealed against or the jurisdiction of the court whose
-- src:3358 | decision is appealed against.
@ref s 60B (src:3354-3358)
`s 60B the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    PARTY `s 60B the Court of Appeal`
    SHANT `s 60B reverses or substantially varies the decision, or orders a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `s 60B the Court of Appeal` BECAUSE "the Court of Appeal reversed or varied a decision, or ordered a new trial, on account of an error that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction"

#TRACE `s 60B the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 60B the Court of Appeal` DOES `s 60B reverses or substantially varies the decision, or orders a new trial, on account of an error, defect or irregularity that does not affect the merits or the jurisdiction` AT 1

§§ `s 60C -- Appeal not to operate as stay of execution or enforcement`

-- src:3364 | 60C.—(1) An appeal to the Court of Appeal does not operate as a
-- src:3365 | stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision
-- src:3366 | appealed against, unless the court whose decision is appealed against
-- src:3367 | or the Court of Appeal so orders.
@ref s 60C(1) (src:3364-3367)
GIVEN `the court whose decision is appealed against or the Court of Appeal has ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60C(1) the appeal operates as a stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision appealed against` MEANS
    IF `the court whose decision is appealed against or the Court of Appeal has ordered a stay`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless" -- the unless is met, which lifts the bar; the text does not say what the order has the effect of
    THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An appeal to the Court of Appeal does not operate as a stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision appealed against"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3364 | 60C.—(1) An appeal to the Court of Appeal does not operate as a
-- src:3365 | stay of execution or enforcement or of proceedings under the decision
-- src:3366 | appealed against, unless the court whose decision is appealed against
-- src:3367 | or the Court of Appeal so orders.
@ref s 60C(1) (src:3364-3367)
GIVEN `the court whose decision is appealed against or the Court of Appeal has ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60C(1) the rule that the appeal does not operate as a stay applies` MEANS
    IF `the court whose decision is appealed against or the Court of Appeal has ordered a stay`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless ... so orders" -- the unless is met, so the rule does not apply
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:3373 | (2) No intermediate act or proceeding is to be invalidated except so
-- src:3374 | far as the Court of Appeal directs.
@ref s 60C(2) (src:3373-3374)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60C(2) the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "except so far as the Court of Appeal directs" -- the exception is met; the text does not say more
    THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No intermediate act or proceeding is to be invalidated except so far as the Court of Appeal directs."
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3373 | (2) No intermediate act or proceeding is to be invalidated except so
-- src:3374 | far as the Court of Appeal directs.
@ref s 60C(2) (src:3373-3374)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60C(2) the rule that no intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated applies` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal directs that the intermediate act or proceeding is invalidated`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except so far as the Court of Appeal directs" -- the exception is met, so the rule does not apply
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE
